00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:08.580
at some random, weird
part of Kubernetes?

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:09.460
GKE?

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:10.240
Tell us about it.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:10.920
What do you do?

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:11.961
What do you do at Google?

00:06:11.961 --> 00:06:13.000
DANIEL SMITH: Hi.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.450
Yeah, honestly I'm not sure
what TL stands for either.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.900
So I'm the TL of
the API Machinery

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.920
subteam at Kubernetes.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.310
As the Kubernetes
team has grown,

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:28.810
we've broken it into a bunch of
small groups, small-ish groups.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:32.140
So my group is the
API Machinery group.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:36.130
And our goal is to take
care of the API Machinery.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:40.720
So this is the stuff,
the guts of Kubernetes,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.480
the plumbing between
clients and servers

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:47.260
and between the server
and storage backend.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.430
Making client libraries, making
the stuff that lets people

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:52.141
write new APIs.

