00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:36.760
DANIEL SMITH: If you look
at our documentation,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.410
we've got a number of areas.

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:39.930
We've increased the scale.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.850
Federation is to beta.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:44.030
RBAC is to beta.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.840
RBAC is a security feature.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:48.490
There's some new
scheduling tricks

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.002
that you can do, a
few other things.

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:51.460
MARK MANDEL: Let's
talk about scale

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.584
because obviously scale
and Federation-- there seem

00:07:53.584 --> 00:07:54.970
to be two fairly hot topics.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.374
I believe Kubernetes now
goes up to 5,000 nodes.

00:07:57.374 --> 00:07:58.040
Is that correct?

00:07:58.040 --> 00:07:59.530
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah,
that's correct.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:02.100
So we now enforce our--

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:06.730
we promise our SLO over 5,000
nodes, 5,000 node clusters.

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:10.780
This and the SLO is
the control plane

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.377
responds to API requests in
under a second 99% of the time.

00:08:13.377 --> 00:08:14.710
MARK MANDEL: I was going to say.

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:15.880
So that's interesting.

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:18.400
Can you tell us actually
how that was possible?

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.870
What actually changed?

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.280
Are there changes
under the hood?

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.620
Or what happened there so
that that would be possible

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.150
so that that can happen?

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.090
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah,
the scalability team

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:31.180
made a bunch of changes
to make this happen.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:35.450
One of the larger ones is
switching to etcd version 3.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.630
We were previously on etcd 2.

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.360
That was actually a
pretty large effort.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.659
That was done by
Wojciech in Poland

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:46.787
and my own team member, Matt,
here put in a lot of work

00:08:46.787 --> 00:08:47.620
getting that set up.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:50.940
And we had lots of help
from friends at CoreOS.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:55.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Is
it true that etcd 3--

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.490
one of the biggest changes
is that it actually

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:00.160
uses gRPC instead of REST?

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.210
Is that what you think
brought the performance gain?

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:11.480
So that's actually a detail
about the etcd client library.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:15.670
DANIEL SMITH: So yes,
it does use gRPC.

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:17.800
But we actually got
that benefit just

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:19.370
by switching to
the client library.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:25.837
So etcd itself scales better if
you use the version 3 format.

00:09:25.837 --> 00:09:26.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, nice.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:28.160
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.620
And we know Federation's a
pretty big thing in Kubernetes.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:34.430
Can you tell us A, what is
Kubernetes Federation, and B,

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:37.937
what's new in 1.6 that we
can take advantage of now?

00:09:37.937 --> 00:09:40.270
DANIEL SMITH: After you get
a cluster to a certain size,

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:43.540
it becomes very problematic if
something goes wrong, right?

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:45.820
So after a certain
size, it's better

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:47.875
to start running
multiple clusters.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.110
You don't want the blast radius
to be too big if something

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:54.318
happens to your control plan.

00:09:54.318 --> 00:09:57.430
So Federation is a way of
presenting multiple clusters

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.320
under a single control plane.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:00.140
It's not new.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.920
We've had it for a while,
but it is growing up.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:05.350
And I think it went to
beta this time around.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.710
And it can do some things
like manage pods for you,

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.210
manage replication controllers.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:11.740
I think it's got services.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:14.710
So you can manage multiple
clusters at the same time.

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How does it
look when you use Federation?

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:21.260
Does that mean that
kubectl has a special mode?

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:25.890
So when you deploy it to the
world, what does it look like?

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:28.390
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah, so I'm a
little bit in danger of telling

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:29.348
you stuff I don't know.

00:10:29.348 --> 00:10:33.670
But you point your
Kube control--

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.740
you set your kubeconfig file to
point to the Federation control

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:37.240
plane.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.120
And it's got its own
set of controllers that

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:42.110
just maintain configuration.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:47.410
Say you want a service to
run in multiple clusters.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:51.190
It looks just like the regular
config file from Kubernetes.

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:53.500
And there's maybe a
couple extra fields

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.207
that help it find
how many clusters

00:10:56.207 --> 00:10:57.790
and which clusters
you want it to run.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:58.706
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:10:58.706 --> 00:11:00.760
So I guess that
since we've talked

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:05.350
about how you can run bigger
jobs through both larger

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:08.080
clusters and also many
clusters through Federation,

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:11.480
the next thing that
you mentioned was RBAC.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:12.490
What is RBAC?

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:13.512
What does it stand for?

00:11:13.512 --> 00:11:15.220
DANIEL SMITH: Ah, what
does it stand for?

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:16.344
How about I tell you what--

00:11:16.344 --> 00:11:16.902
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:16.902 --> 00:11:18.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sounds good.

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:19.810
MARK MANDEL: Is it
role-based access control?

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:21.726
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah,
role-based access control?

00:11:21.726 --> 00:11:22.850
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:26.620
So basically Kubernetes
is starting to grow up.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:29.170
And we're getting more
fine-grained control

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:30.740
over security.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:33.190
When people think of
security, I think there's

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.380
two topics that that covers.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.520
One is identity or what
we call authentication.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:43.240
And the other is permissions
or what we call authorization.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.810
And so RBAC is
about authorization.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.290
It is a way of
specifying really, really

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:53.155
fine-grained permissions
on various aspects

00:11:53.155 --> 00:11:53.990
of your clusters.

00:11:53.990 --> 00:12:01.530
So you can specify a resource,
a verb, and an actor, a person

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:02.980
or a group.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:05.427
And say, yes, you can do this.

00:12:05.427 --> 00:12:06.010
No, you can't.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:09.730
And this is more fine-grained
than we've had previously.

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:11.620
Well, I think we've
had RBAC previously,

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.385
but it's going to
beta this time around.

00:12:13.385 --> 00:12:15.760
MARK MANDEL: So it's like
IAM but for Kubernetes

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:16.350
specifically.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:17.860
DANIEL SMITH:
Yeah, it is exactly

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.540
like IAM for Kubernetes.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:20.684
It is more fine-grained.

00:12:20.684 --> 00:12:21.600
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.980
So if I'm a large
organization and I

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:26.500
want to have certain,
maybe, senior developers who

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.060
are able to push to production.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:29.560
But maybe my more
junior people I'm

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:31.589
going to let them push
to dev or staging.

00:12:31.589 --> 00:12:33.130
Then I've got controls
there that I'm

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:34.630
able to take advantage
of to do that.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:35.921
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah, absolutely.

00:12:35.921 --> 00:12:38.259
MARK MANDEL: What's the
authorization hooks there?

00:12:38.259 --> 00:12:40.300
Should I be able to hook
them into, I don't know,

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:43.150
an LDAP system or my own
custom authentication?

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:44.582
Or what's available there?

00:12:44.582 --> 00:12:46.540
DANIEL SMITH: Kubernetes
supports multiple ways

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:47.420
of getting identity.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:49.990
So LDAP will give you identity.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.070
If you're running
a GKE installation,

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:55.390
then you get identity
from the Google system.

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:58.870
One of the things we added is
a subject access review API.

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.120
So you can actually, if you
have permission to do it,

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:03.220
you can actually call a
Kubernetes control plane

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:05.440
and ask, does this
person have permission

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.094
to do this thing
on this cluster?

00:13:07.094 --> 00:13:08.260
MARK MANDEL: Sure, OK, cool.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.844
So the thing that I'm actually
personally quite excited about,

00:13:10.844 --> 00:13:12.634
and something I've been
playing with a bit,

00:13:12.634 --> 00:13:14.380
is the advanced
scheduling stuff.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:16.430
I think this is
super, super cool.

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:18.582
There's several
different things here.

00:13:18.582 --> 00:13:20.290
Which one do we want
to talk about first?

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:22.030
So there's a whole bunch of
stuff around pod affinity

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:23.140
that I think is
really interesting.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:24.230
Can you tell us about that?

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:26.188
DANIEL SMITH: I was
reading about it last night

00:13:26.188 --> 00:13:27.730
just to make sure.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:28.670
I understood it.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:30.850
There's a couple of
different affinity settings.

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.220
There's affinity for nodes
and affinity for pods.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:38.170
Affinity for nodes is
you'd prefer or you must.

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:40.180
Your pod prefers
a particular node,

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.180
or it must schedule
on a particular node.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.830
Pod affinity is even
more interesting.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:46.480
That is for situations
where you want

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:49.450
to be colocated with
another set of pods,

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:53.170
or you want to be not
colocated with a set of pods.

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.840
So pod affinity is
for when you want

00:13:55.840 --> 00:14:00.820
to schedule taking account
of the other pods that

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:03.920
are on a node or in an
unavailability zone.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:07.210
So it's if you have pods that
need to be scheduled together

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:09.130
or shouldn't be
scheduled together.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:11.770
Suppose you have
workloads that need

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:14.290
high-bandwidth communication
between each other,

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:17.260
like you're doing some
machine learning something.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:20.080
And you want them to be
in a network segment that

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:22.090
has high connectivity.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:25.910
So you can set up
labels on your node

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:28.120
saying what your topology is.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:34.570
And you can select over what
other pods have been scheduled

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:38.020
in this topology section.

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:41.200
And do I want to be scheduled
close to those pods?

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:44.650
Or maybe you want to spread
for disaster tolerance.

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:47.080
You could say, I want
to be scheduled away

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:48.050
from these other pods.

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:50.440
MARK MANDEL: So an
interesting question then.

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:52.360
So I can have
multiple containers

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:54.340
running inside a given pod.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:56.890
Why would I choose
to use pod affinity

00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:59.200
to bring other pods
together versus putting them

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:01.712
all together in the same pod
just with multiple containers?

00:15:01.712 --> 00:15:02.920
What benefits do I get there?

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:04.378
DANIEL SMITH: I
think that's really

00:15:04.378 --> 00:15:07.120
for very different use cases.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.450
If you're putting multiple
things inside one pod,

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:14.700
you're saying that your scaling
is literally one-to-one.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:18.140
If you have, I don't know, a web
frontend and a database syncer

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:22.720
in one pod, you are getting
exactly one of each every time

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.430
you scale up or scale down.

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:27.400
If you have services that
need to scale separately

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.890
but still be colocated,
like maybe you've

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:34.320
got one database that serves
several different web services,

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.580
the services may need to
be close to the database.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:39.550
But you don't want
to scale one-to-one.

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:41.710
So in that case, you want
separate pods for each.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:43.918
MARK MANDEL: So you're
talking about on the pod side,

00:15:43.918 --> 00:15:46.030
you've got ways to
coordinate where

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:49.150
pods should be close to
each other or affinity

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:49.950
against each other.

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:52.851
But then you can also control
things on the node side

00:15:52.851 --> 00:15:53.350
as well.

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:54.100
Is that right?

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:57.490
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah, so
there's a new beta feature--

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:58.690
node taints.

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:02.500
And I like to think of this
as the role-playing game

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:08.750
sense of the node is
tainted by some dark magic.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:12.220
You're basically setting
a flag on the node.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:15.070
And then the system
will prevent any pod

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:18.110
that doesn't tolerate that
flag from landing on the node.

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:20.380
So you could use
this for if you've

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:24.670
got nodes that have
GPUs or a local SSD,

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:27.340
and you want more normal
pods not to schedule

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:31.930
on those because you want to
leave those free for workloads

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:34.580
that really take advantage
of the unique feature.

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:39.190
So you could taint those
nodes with, say, GPU true.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.650
And then only pods that
actually say, I tolerate

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:44.020
the GPS taint can land there.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:47.170
You can use this to do
all sorts of cool stuff.

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:50.270
Along the same lines,
there is an alpha feature.

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:53.620
So the system is continuously
health checking nodes.

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:56.020
And after a while, it will--

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:57.840
if a node stops calling--

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.680
if a node stops phoning
home, the system

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.050
will set its status to unknown.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:06.430
And if it doesn't come back,
it will eventually just

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:08.170
kick all the pods
off of the node.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:13.310
You can now set some flags and
treat that event as a taint

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:13.930
itself.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:17.980
So you can control
how long your pods

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:21.280
are willing to tolerate a node
that is possibly unresponsive.

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:24.010
MARK MANDEL: So the
question I have is

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:25.905
it sounds like pod
affinity and node

00:17:25.905 --> 00:17:27.339
taints kind of do
the same things

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:29.900
but from different directions.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:32.415
When should I choose
one over the other?

00:17:32.415 --> 00:17:38.110
DANIEL SMITH: I think clearly if
you want your pods to do stuff

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:39.910
based on other
pods in the system,

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:42.130
then you definitely want
to use pod affinity.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:44.920
If you want your pods
to schedule depending

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.140
on facts about your
nodes, then you probably

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:48.880
want to use node taints.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.660
The other distinction is
taints are for keeping pods off

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:56.290
of nodes, and affinity is
for getting pods onto nodes.

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:59.770
So if you really want to set
up an exclusive relationship,

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:00.809
you need both.

00:18:00.809 --> 00:18:03.100
MARK MANDEL: All right, so
the final thing on the event

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:05.020
schedule stuff, which
is also interesting,

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:06.850
is custom schedulers.

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:10.450
Up until this point, Kubernetes
has had the scheduler, which

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:12.880
has been the one true way.

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:14.680
But now you can
mess with it a bit.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:15.940
What's that all about?

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:17.710
DANIEL SMITH: So
specifically this feature

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:20.320
is you leave the default
scheduler running,

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:22.930
and you startup your
own scheduler beside it.

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:26.050
You can set with a
field in your pods

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:29.170
which scheduler you would like
your pods to be scheduled by.

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:32.360
And you can do this,
I don't know, maybe

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:37.210
if you've got some special logic
that isn't covered by affinity

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:39.280
or taints or threading.

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:41.539
If you've got something
custom that needs to happen,

00:18:41.539 --> 00:18:43.580
you can write a scheduler
that does that for you.

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:46.030
And then you can have your
pods go through that scheduler

00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:47.334
instead of the default one.

00:18:47.334 --> 00:18:48.750
MARK MANDEL: And
I think I saw one

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:50.170
of our teammates, Ian Lewis--

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:53.819
he'll probably write a blog
post for the Kubernetes blog.

00:18:53.819 --> 00:18:55.360
I will link to it
in the show notes--

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:58.900
wherein I think I saw-- yeah,
he wrote a custom scheduler

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:00.075
in Bash.

00:19:00.075 --> 00:19:02.710
So there doesn't seem to
be a limit on the language

00:19:02.710 --> 00:19:05.500
that you can write your
custom schedulers in.

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:07.061
Is that correct?

00:19:07.061 --> 00:19:09.310
DANIEL SMITH: One of the
best things about Kubernetes,

00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:13.820
I think, is just the fact
that our APIs are truly open.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:17.140
There's no secret API.

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:19.750
All of the stuff that the
default system components do

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:20.770
is stuff you can do.

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:23.470
And you can do it through
the Go client library.

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:26.230
You can do it through
Kube control and Bash.

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:28.270
The system doesn't
really care what's

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:29.830
on the other end of the API.

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:33.010
We're starting to come out with
libraries and other languages.

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:36.250
There's a pretty full-featured
Python library now.

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:38.830
So yeah, you can
write it in Bash.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:41.800
I wouldn't recommend it, but if
that's what floats your boat.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:42.716
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:42.716 --> 00:19:45.910
So I had a question regarding
not scheduling anymore.

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:49.090
But sometimes you want
to scale your cluster.

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:50.440
You need more volumes.

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:51.700
You need storage.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:54.100
The only way to create
this is either you

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:56.320
create it by
yourself or you want

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.720
to create it automatically.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:03.375
And I think in 1.4, dynamic
provisioning was added.

00:20:03.375 --> 00:20:05.234
And there's something
new about it,

00:20:05.234 --> 00:20:06.400
but I don't know what it is.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.440
What's going on with
dynamic storage provision?

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:12.511
DANIEL SMITH: So I think this
feature is pretty awesome.

00:20:12.511 --> 00:20:14.260
Let me give some
background on the storage

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:15.640
system in Kubernetes.

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:17.320
Because a large
part of Kubernetes

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:19.090
is workload portability, right?

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:22.840
We want to be able to write
your application targeted

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:25.840
at Kubernetes and then run it
in multiple cloud environments.

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.660
Now it turns out that cloud
environments don't all

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:30.730
do storage volumes the same way.

00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:33.970
So to get around this,
we have a concept

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:35.920
which is persistent volume.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:39.340
And we have another concept
called persistent volume claim.

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:41.470
And the idea is you
write your workload

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:44.380
to talk to a persistent
volume claim.

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:47.230
And you allocate
persistent volumes

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:49.250
from the cloud provider.

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:51.560
So if you go to a
different cloud provider,

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.260
all that has to
change is the logic

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:54.730
that's producing the volumes.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:57.550
Your application
doesn't even notice.

00:20:57.550 --> 00:21:00.850
And so what's new
here is that there's

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:02.650
a storage class
that's introduced,

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:03.890
a storage class object.

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:07.450
And that is basically a
thing that can automatically

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:09.690
produce persistent volumes.

00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:11.740
So it frees cluster
administrators

00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:12.940
from having to do that.

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:13.856
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:21:13.856 --> 00:21:19.630
So I'm curious about you
mentioned the storage claim.

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:22.690
Could that be seen
as basically the API

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:27.580
that someone needs to satisfy
to be able to provide storage

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.590
volumes for Kubernetes?

00:21:29.590 --> 00:21:32.920
Which means that basically
there's one for Google Cloud.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.620
But also if you want to run
it on other cloud providers

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:38.835
or even on premise, that's what
you should be implementing?

00:21:38.835 --> 00:21:42.460
DANIEL SMITH: So if you
wanted to port Kubernetes

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:44.130
to a particular
cloud environment,

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:49.930
the thing that you want to
produce is a controller logic.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:55.150
So I haven't looked at the
dynamic storage provisioning

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:57.910
feature at code level.

00:21:57.910 --> 00:22:01.360
But there must be a controller.

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:06.735
And if you wanted to port to
a different cloud environment,

00:22:06.735 --> 00:22:08.110
you would write
an implementation

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:10.870
of that controller
Call that would

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:15.100
look for unsatisfied claims
against a storage class.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.949
And then you'd select
the appropriate volume

00:22:17.949 --> 00:22:20.240
settings, size, et cetera,
from your cloud environment.

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:21.670
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:23.550
Is there any other--

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:25.300
those seem to be the
big highlights that I

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:26.090
think we've covered there.

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:27.910
But are there any
other little things

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:30.279
that have also been added
that are cool and interesting?

00:22:30.279 --> 00:22:32.320
DANIEL SMITH: Some
under-the-hood stuff, I think,

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:33.850
is probably worth mentioning.

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:37.870
A bunch of work has been done
on something called a Container

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:38.800
Runtime Interface.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:43.960
And this effort is probably
not super user-visible,

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:47.470
but it will make some things
easier for us in the future.

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:49.840
And basically the
idea is kubelet,

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:54.530
which is the node agent, has
two responsibilities basically.

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:57.730
On one side, it's talking
to the Kubernetes API

00:22:57.730 --> 00:23:00.190
and getting workloads and
reporting back, et cetera.

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:02.650
And on the other side, it's
talking to the container

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:04.870
runtime, which is
usually Docker,

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:07.600
and is starting
containers, stopping them.

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:10.810
So the Container Runtime
Interface's effort

00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:15.520
is to split this down the middle
and let the container runtime

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:19.690
part of that be out of process
to kubelet so that it's easier

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:21.530
for people to implement that.

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:25.150
Kubelet can focus on correctly
implementing the Kubernetes

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:26.535
API.

00:23:26.535 --> 00:23:29.360
Another cool thing
that kubelet is doing--

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.570
I'm excited about
this because it

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:34.820
is fixing a longstanding
issue that we've had

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:37.690
sometimes-- is kubelet
is now declaring

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:39.340
space for system daemons.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:41.820
I call this feature
node allocatable.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:45.340
And the idea is the
system components,

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:48.701
like kubelet itself,
Docker, other daemons

00:23:48.701 --> 00:23:50.950
that are running on the
system, they take up some RAM.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:52.540
They take up some CPU.

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:54.230
And if you don't take
that into account,

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:56.500
the scheduler will stuff
so much stuff onto the node

00:23:56.500 --> 00:24:00.280
that it will go out of memory
and start killing things.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.990
So it's pretty cool to have
this put in to the node stats

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:06.770
so that the scheduler
can take account

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:09.490
to this and not overload nodes.

00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:10.480
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:12.896
Now I think we've covered all
the stuff that's pretty much

00:24:12.896 --> 00:24:14.120
available and in beta.

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:17.184
But we've had some new alpha
features as well that--

00:24:17.184 --> 00:24:19.600
I know we don't necessarily
support out-of-the-box without

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:21.340
clicking some
checkboxes and saying,

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:25.165
this is fine if you
set yourself on fire.

00:24:25.165 --> 00:24:26.290
Do you want to talk about--

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:28.550
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah, you can make
an alpha cluster, but you can--

00:24:28.550 --> 00:24:29.674
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, exactly.

00:24:29.674 --> 00:24:31.050
It's all on you.

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:33.471
But yeah, what new alpha
features are available

00:24:33.471 --> 00:24:33.970
right now?

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:37.000
DANIEL SMITH: So one thing I'm
excited about is out-of-tree

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:40.420
cloud providers, which
is we're splitting our--

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:43.226
there's multiple control plane
components in Kubernetes.

00:24:43.226 --> 00:24:45.100
And one of them is called
controller manager.

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:47.864
That groups all of the
various controllers

00:24:47.864 --> 00:24:49.030
that are running the system.

00:24:49.030 --> 00:24:51.310
Kubernetes does this "build
out of this control loop"

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:53.950
pattern where we have
these components that

00:24:53.950 --> 00:24:57.190
basically are doing one thing.

00:24:57.190 --> 00:25:00.460
They're watching [INAUDIBLE]
said, it wants five pods.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:01.840
They're looking at the cluster.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:02.770
You only have four.

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:03.810
So it'll make a new one.

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:06.210
The whole system is built
out of this control loop.

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:08.250
We're splitting
that binary up so

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:13.320
that the ones that are specific
and married to a cloud provider

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:14.790
are in a different binary.

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:18.600
So that you can run the standard
controller manager binary

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:22.945
and also the one appropriate
for your cloud environment.

00:25:22.945 --> 00:25:25.320
If you were doing a bare metal
installation or something,

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:27.111
you could just take a
look at that skeleton

00:25:27.111 --> 00:25:29.770
and see what it provides and
write your own implementation.

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:33.720
Another thing I'm excited
about, which is alpha support,

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:35.040
is GPUs.

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:38.490
So we handle GPUs as
resources on nodes now.

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:40.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Could
you tell me more about it?

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:44.640
What does it look like to
say, I want to use GPUs.

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:48.030
Let's say there's someone that
wants to do video game stuff,

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:49.860
and they need video
rendering of stuff.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.030
How does it look?

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:53.530
DANIEL SMITH: I haven't played
with that particular feature,

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:55.800
so I am probably not
the best person to ask.

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:58.250
I thought it was targeted more
towards machine learning--

00:25:58.250 --> 00:25:58.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Oh, interesting.

00:25:58.850 --> 00:26:00.090
DANIEL SMITH: --folks.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:03.240
It probably also works for
people who need to do renders.

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:05.370
But I think it's
targeted towards--

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:05.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's a good point.

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:06.360
DANIEL SMITH:
--machine learning.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:09.500
MARK MANDEL: So more exposure
of the CUDA stuff and that stuff

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:11.264
and NVIDIA cards and
things like that.

00:26:11.264 --> 00:26:12.930
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah,
I don't know that we

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:14.420
go into that level of detail.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:18.140
I think it's just the
kubelet notes that, hey, I've

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:19.830
got this many GPUs available.

00:26:19.830 --> 00:26:21.640
And then the scheduler
can see that pods

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:27.150
want one, two, a
few GPUs, and it

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:28.455
can track that as a resource.

00:26:28.455 --> 00:26:29.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:30.731
One more question
I wanted to ask you

00:26:30.731 --> 00:26:33.660
is do you know a
little bit about how

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:36.000
was it to migrate to Go 1.8.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:37.990
Was it an easy thing?

00:26:37.990 --> 00:26:41.760
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah, so we
did just get to Go 1.8.

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:46.140
I think we got there yesterday,
maybe the day before.

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:51.150
So Kubernetes 1.6 is
still built with 1.7.

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:55.480
But on head, we are on Go 1.8.

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:57.800
And it seems cool so far.

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:00.360
I've been running it on my
personal system for a while.

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:02.040
I'm excited to see more.

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:02.975
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:27:02.975 --> 00:27:04.350
We are running
out of time a bit.

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:07.710
But what's on the roadmap
for Kubernetes going forward?

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:09.780
I saw there have
been drops on 1.7

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:11.105
for alpha releases and stuff.

00:27:11.105 --> 00:27:13.230
But what stuff can we see
coming down the pipeline?

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:17.100
DANIEL SMITH: I'll just
focus on what I and my team

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:17.940
are working on.

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:21.855
I think we've got some
cool stuff planned.

00:27:21.855 --> 00:27:24.760
The things that get me
excited in Kubernetes

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:27.390
are the things that make
it easier for the ecosystem

00:27:27.390 --> 00:27:31.170
to develop and for us to
get more components that

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.180
interoperate with Kubernetes.

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:37.680
And so in that vein, we're
working on API aggregation.

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:41.670
This is the idea that
you can take your own API

00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:44.740
and aggregate it seamlessly
with the Kubernetes API.

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:48.240
So we have various
folks in the ecosystem,

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:52.430
like OpenShift,
who basically are

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:54.930
operating something that's very,
very similar to Kubernetes.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:59.310
But it's actually a fork
if you look under the hood.

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:02.640
They have very
painful merges when

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:05.390
Kubernetes increments version.

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:08.460
So the idea here
is the people who

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:13.650
want to add this sort of API
can run it on top of Kubernetes

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.750
instead of operating a fork.

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.300
And this should make it
much easier to add APIs.

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:20.130
You should be able
to add multiple APIs

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:21.520
in the same cluster.

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:23.400
So yeah, this is what
I'm looking forward to.

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:25.940
MARK MANDEL: Is that linked
to third-party resources?

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:27.839
Is that in that
same vein as people

00:28:27.839 --> 00:28:29.880
who want to add their own
resources to Kubernetes

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:31.129
and make it do special things?

00:28:31.129 --> 00:28:32.830
DANIEL SMITH: Yeah,
it is similar.

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:36.270
And this is a really
hot topic last quarter.

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:39.769
If you poke around the
API Machinery SIG docs,

00:28:39.769 --> 00:28:42.060
you'll find one that I put
out there that was comparing

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:43.350
and contrasting the two.

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:46.020
I think the short story
is that Kubernetes

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:48.480
needs both third-party
resources and API

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:50.340
aggregation going forward.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:53.670
Third-party resources-- the
target audience there is it's

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.800
really easy to get
something up and running.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.080
It doesn't take a lot of effort.

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:00.550
It works with Kube
control already.

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:06.810
So it's low effort, easy to use,
not necessarily full-featured.

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:10.206
And the idea with
aggregation, API aggregation,

00:29:10.206 --> 00:29:11.580
is a little bit
more heavyweight.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:13.121
Probably you're only
going to do this

00:29:13.121 --> 00:29:16.965
if you really want to
fundamentally alter or add

00:29:16.965 --> 00:29:21.540
some really fundamental
API concept.

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:23.910
We're going to offer a library.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:24.630
You'd compile.

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:26.550
You'd run your own API server.

00:29:26.550 --> 00:29:29.220
You make an entry in
the aggregation API.

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:31.790
And then that would get
integrated with Kube control.

00:29:31.790 --> 00:29:33.331
And if you do things
that way, you'll

00:29:33.331 --> 00:29:36.060
get all the power
of the API systems

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:39.850
so you can do all the
fancy validation you want.

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:42.600
You can write super custom
admission controller.

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:46.592
You can do strange
defaulting, anything you like.

00:29:46.592 --> 00:29:47.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:29:47.550 --> 00:29:50.090
I think that we're pretty
out of time by now.

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:52.110
So thank you so much
for taking the time

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.810
to tell us a little bit about
all the cool things that

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:56.930
come in with Kubernetes 1.6.

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:57.930
It was very interesting.

00:29:57.930 --> 00:29:58.320
DANIEL SMITH: Cool.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:29:58.890
Thanks for having me.

00:29:58.890 --> 00:29:59.850
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
thank you so much.

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:01.308
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So thanks, Daniel,

00:30:01.308 --> 00:30:03.370
for such an interesting
conversation.

00:30:03.370 --> 00:30:05.710
There was a lot of those
things that I'm not

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:07.930
sure I completely
understood what

00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:11.170
they were for
because like there's

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:12.520
really advanced stuff there.

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:15.790
If you've felt that
way, do not fear.

00:30:15.790 --> 00:30:17.330
That's normal I guess.

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:18.400
But thanks.

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:19.020
Yeah, thanks.

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:20.436
It was really,
really interesting,

00:30:20.436 --> 00:30:22.887
and it makes you think about
all of those little features

00:30:22.887 --> 00:30:24.220
that probably you will not need.

00:30:24.220 --> 00:30:26.120
But the day you'll need
them, they're there.

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:26.870
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:29.670
So why don't we have a chat
about the question of the week?

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:31.840
I thought this would be a
bit interesting if we just

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:33.160
talked a little bit about.

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:36.250
So you create a Google
Cloud Platform project.

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:37.780
What's the first thing you do?

00:30:37.780 --> 00:30:39.694
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Let
me say my things first.

00:30:39.694 --> 00:30:40.360
MARK MANDEL: Go.

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:42.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
normally what I do

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:44.260
is I create my
projects all the time

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:46.285
from the console, from
console.cloud.google.com.

00:30:46.285 --> 00:30:47.410
MARK MANDEL: I do the same.

00:30:47.410 --> 00:30:49.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And then what
I do is I go to my terminal,

00:30:49.900 --> 00:30:52.930
and I do gcloud init to set
up all the configuration

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:55.360
so whenever I do
anything from gcloud,

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:57.800
it will be actually
referring to that.

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:02.170
And I do this partly because I
have three different accounts

00:31:02.170 --> 00:31:03.480
linked to my gcloud.

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:06.490
So I want to make sure that
I do what I do in the project

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:07.430
that I'm trying to.

00:31:07.430 --> 00:31:09.600
Otherwise, I would get
errors, and it's annoying.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:11.560
MARK MANDEL: Do you
use configurations?

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:14.595
Or do you just read reauth
on the same account?

00:31:14.595 --> 00:31:16.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I
do not use configurations.

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:17.560
I just do reauth.

00:31:17.560 --> 00:31:18.910
And it's very easy.

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:20.050
It works pretty well.

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:21.970
There's a product
manager for gcloud here.

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:24.345
I wish that I could
have directory-dependent

00:31:24.345 --> 00:31:24.970
configurations.

00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:26.303
MARK MANDEL: That would be nice.

00:31:26.303 --> 00:31:28.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Consider
it as a feature request

00:31:28.950 --> 00:31:29.930
from the podcast.

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:32.500
But yeah, just gcloud init,
and that works pretty well.

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:34.540
And then, well,
right after that,

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:37.150
I'm going to go to
the console again.

00:31:37.150 --> 00:31:38.770
And it seems very
often what I'm doing

00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:42.460
is I'm going to be running
somehow Compute Engine

00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:43.800
instances.

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:46.600
And you need to
activate that API.

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:49.480
What I do is I go to
the Compute Engine tab.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:53.320
And then just by clicking there,
the API starts activating.

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:54.980
That takes a couple of minutes.

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:56.950
So it's always the
first thing you do.

00:31:56.950 --> 00:32:00.705
You don't pay for
having me activate it.

00:32:00.705 --> 00:32:02.080
Sometimes I deploy
in App Engine,

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:03.497
and I still will
activate the API.

00:32:03.497 --> 00:32:04.621
MARK MANDEL: Just by habit.

00:32:04.621 --> 00:32:07.197
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, just
because that's what I do.

00:32:07.197 --> 00:32:08.530
But yeah, that's pretty much it.

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:10.490
And then check that
the billing account

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:11.870
is set up and all
of these things

00:32:11.870 --> 00:32:14.110
so I can actually start working
on the project directly.

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:14.735
What about you?

00:32:14.735 --> 00:32:17.380
MARK MANDEL: So I'm very
similar to you in that I set up

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:19.872
my project through
console.cloud.google.com

00:32:19.872 --> 00:32:20.580
strangely enough.

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:22.791
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You don't
use the deployment manager

00:32:22.791 --> 00:32:23.290
for that?

00:32:23.290 --> 00:32:24.430
MARK MANDEL: So you could.

00:32:24.430 --> 00:32:24.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You could.

00:32:24.820 --> 00:32:25.130
I know.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:25.500
That's why--

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:26.470
DANIEL SMITH: You can.

00:32:26.470 --> 00:32:27.761
I haven't played with that yet.

00:32:27.761 --> 00:32:28.854
But I may one day soon.

00:32:28.854 --> 00:32:29.770
In fact, maybe I will.

00:32:29.770 --> 00:32:31.630
Maybe I will do that now
that I think about it

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:32.754
because I have a few steps.

00:32:32.754 --> 00:32:34.390
So the first thing
I do, actually,

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:37.330
is I make sure I have multiple
owners on the account.

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:39.179
I start with my email
address, and then I

00:32:39.179 --> 00:32:40.720
have a secondary
email address that I

00:32:40.720 --> 00:32:43.094
use to make sure there are
multiple emails on the account

00:32:43.094 --> 00:32:43.874
just in case.

00:32:43.874 --> 00:32:46.540
Always a good thing to have just
in case one account-- something

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:47.164
happens to it.

00:32:47.164 --> 00:32:49.330
And then you can still have
access to your projects.

00:32:49.330 --> 00:32:51.670
Ideally multiple
people is a good idea,

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:53.450
but yeah, multiple
owners is always good.

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:56.380
And then basically-- this
is not really a step but--

00:32:56.380 --> 00:32:57.790
make sure that the emails--

00:32:57.790 --> 00:32:59.141
the owners on my accounts--

00:32:59.141 --> 00:33:00.640
I make sure those
are email accounts

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:02.980
that I actually look
at on a regular basis

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:05.420
because if anything goes
bad with your project--

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:07.930
In fact, we had a conversation
with the abuse team

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:08.660
at one point.

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:10.410
If anything bad happens
with your project,

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:12.826
say, somebody compromises one
of your systems or something

00:33:12.826 --> 00:33:15.770
like that happens, then that's
the email address you're

00:33:15.770 --> 00:33:17.080
going to get a message out to.

00:33:17.080 --> 00:33:18.080
So you want to receive those.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:19.560
Those are really important
because otherwise you

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:21.830
can get your project shut
down, and that would be sad.

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:23.455
And that would make
you very, very sad.

00:33:23.455 --> 00:33:25.570
So definitely I like
having multiple owners

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:27.175
and making sure that those
emails are monitored.

00:33:27.175 --> 00:33:29.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Going back
to the topic you were talking

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:31.180
about abuse and support.

00:33:31.180 --> 00:33:33.820
Yeah, reading those
emails is really important

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:35.870
because when you receive
one of those emails,

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:38.800
they're actually important,
and you need to answer them.

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:41.260
There's two episodes where we
cover a little bit about this

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:43.630
and the best practices
on how to make sure

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:46.150
that your projects are
following the terms of services

00:33:46.150 --> 00:33:47.820
and all of these things.

00:33:47.820 --> 00:33:50.350
There's episode 47
with cloud abuse.

00:33:50.350 --> 00:33:54.550
And we had Swati Kulshreshth
and Emeka Okonkwo.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:58.780
And then on episode 24, we
also had Terrance Shepherd

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:00.880
talking about Google
Cloud Platform support.

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:02.737
Yeah, we covered all
the little things

00:34:02.737 --> 00:34:03.820
that you might want to do.

00:34:03.820 --> 00:34:05.980
Especially I'd say that
the biggest piece of advice

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:07.820
is make sure those
emails do not go

00:34:07.820 --> 00:34:09.232
to some account nobody reads.

00:34:09.232 --> 00:34:11.190
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, don't
send them to dev/null.

00:34:11.190 --> 00:34:11.773
Don't do that.

00:34:11.773 --> 00:34:12.790
That's bad.

